from authoring import finish,ref,step,paragraph
finish('higher-order-recursion-scheme-tree-equivalence',
 id='TCS-6582',title='Equivalence of deterministic higher-order recursion schemes',area='Automata and formal languages',criterion='decision',question_type='yes_no',
 formal='Is there a Turing algorithm that, on every pair of finite deterministic higher-order recursion schemes G,H over the same finite ranked alphabet, halts and decides whether their possibly infinite value trees are exactly equal, including divergence leaves?',
 definitions='Types are generated by o and τ→σ. A terminal a of rank r has type o→⋯→o with r arguments. A scheme has finitely many typed nonterminals, a start S:o, and exactly one rule F x₁⋯x_k→t for each F:τ₁→⋯→τ_k→o. The distinct parameters x_i have the indicated types; t:o is a well-typed applicative term made from terminals, nonterminals and those parameters. There is no pattern matching, test on constructor values, nondeterministic choice, or restriction called safety. To obtain the value at a node, unfold the head nonterminal with substitution until a terminal a is exposed, then repeat for its r ordered arguments. If no terminal is ever exposed, that node is a nullary ⊥ leaf. This defines a tree over the alphabet extended with ⊥. Equality means identical node domains and labels at every finite address in the ordered tree; evaluation-step counts are ignored. The order of o is 0 and ord(τ→σ)=max(ord(τ)+1,ord(σ)); the maximum nonterminal order is finite but part of the input. Inputs may have different orders. No time bound is imposed beyond halting on every valid pair.',
 answer_criterion='An affirmative answer is a single effective decision procedure for all finite input orders, with a proof of termination and correctness on equal as well as unequal trees. A negative answer proves this decision problem undecidable. Decidable model checking of one generated tree, a bounded-order subclass, or a conditional reduction to another open problem is not a full resolution.',
 context=[
 paragraph('A finite recursive program can describe an infinite object without listing its nodes. The rule S→a S, for unary a, describes an endless chain of a labels. The two-rule system S→a T and T→a S describes exactly the same chain even though its internal call structure differs. This card asks whether such equality of the entire observable unfolding can always be decided when functions may also be passed as arguments.'),
 paragraph('The output is an uninterpreted constructor tree. A binary constructor named plus is merely a label with two ordered children; the model does not add the arithmetic equation plus(0,x)=x. Likewise, it does not include a program context allowed to inspect generated values. This narrow semantics matters because equivalence questions for richer programming languages can be undecidable for reasons absent from recursion schemes.'),
 paragraph('Divergence is also observable in a precise way. The rule S→S generates ⊥, while S→a S generates an infinite productive chain. A scheme S→b S c, with binary b and nullary c, can expose infinitely many labels despite never completing its left subtree. Head unfolding therefore differs from an evaluation convention that insists on fully evaluating every argument before producing a constructor.'),
 paragraph('At order zero, the generated trees are regular and equality can be checked by comparing finite state descriptions. At order one, the problem connects to deterministic pushdown-language equivalence, whose decidability follows from Sénizergues’s theorem. Allowing higher-order parameters makes the finite description much more expressive. The inherited Hague–Murawski–Ong–Serre paper identifies equivalence as a central remaining question.','primary'),
 paragraph('That paper’s positive equivalence theorem concerns two kinds of representation: higher-order recursion schemes and collapsible pushdown automata generate the same tree class, with effective translations. It transfers the problem to language equivalence for deterministic collapsible pushdown automata. It does not give an algorithm that compares arbitrary trees represented in either formalism. The difference between representation equivalence and instance equivalence is essential here.','primary'),
 paragraph('Powerful properties of a single tree are nevertheless decidable. Broadbent–Carayol–Ong–Serre provide effective model checking, logical reflection and selection for monadic second-order logic and the modal μ-calculus. Reflection can enrich node labels with the truth of a chosen logical property. Having this machinery does not automatically turn two unrelated infinite descriptions into one object in the same decidable class on which all corresponding nodes can be compared.','logical'),
 paragraph('Jochems supplies a route through coinductive higher-order constrained Horn clauses. The translation represents the generated tree using predicates interpreted through a greatest model and creates separate positive and negative instances. A suitable semidecision procedure for the resulting coinductive problems would yield a decision procedure by interleaving the two searches. The needed semidecidability is left open, so this is a reduction rather than a completed decision algorithm.','horn'),
 paragraph('The same work handles divergence constructively: it transforms a scheme so that each ⊥ leaf is replaced by an infinite chain over a fresh unary symbol. Equality is preserved and reflected. Thus treating divergence carefully need not restrict the question to an informal assumption that every program is productive. The present formulation keeps divergence explicit so readers can compare two given schemes without an unstated promise.','horn'),
 paragraph('Recent results with similar terminology concern other targets. Bojańczyk–Fabiański–Stefański’s April 2026 undecidability theorem compares functions at higher-order types in the polyregular λ-calculus, a language with list-processing operations and extensional input/output semantics. Its theorem is not a stated undecidability result for the closed constructor-tree generators defined above. No reduction identifying the two decision problems is assumed here.','polyregular'),
 paragraph('Dal Lago–Fiorillo–Pistone’s LICS 2026 work instead decides almost-sure termination for a typed subclass of probabilistic recursion schemes, using algebraic generating functions. That achievement concerns termination probabilities under additional type restrictions. The inherited question asks exact equality of deterministic value trees at unrestricted finite orders, and remains separate.','probabilistic')],
 why='A fundamental decidability boundary for comparing higher-order recursive computations: expressive tree generators admit strong verification procedures, while exact equality of two generated behaviors remains unresolved.',
 importance=dict(score=95,method='editorial',assessed_on='2026-09-11',reason='A central equivalence problem joining higher-order functional computation, infinite-tree semantics, and deterministic collapsible pushdown automata.'),
 progress=[step('1997–2002','Sénizergues establishes deterministic pushdown equivalence, giving the order-one recursion-scheme boundary described in the inherited survey.','primary'),step('2008–2017','Hague–Murawski–Ong–Serre give effective translations between recursion schemes and collapsible pushdown automata; the journal article explicitly retains instance equivalence as open.','primary'),step('2021-03-02','Broadbent–Carayol–Ong–Serre’s revision develops effective logical reflection, selection and model checking.','logical'),step('2021','Jochems reduces scheme equivalence to an open coinductive-Horn semidecidability problem and gives an equality-preserving removal of divergence leaves.','horn'),step('2026-04-13','Higher-order polyregular functional equivalence is proved undecidable in a different model and semantics.','polyregular'),step('2026','LICS work gives almost-sure termination decidability for a typed probabilistic subclass, not unrestricted deterministic tree equivalence.','probabilistic')],
 references=[ref('primary','Collapsible Pushdown Automata and Recursion Schemes','Matthew Hague; Andrzej S. Murawski; C.-H. Luke Ong; Olivier Serre',2017,'https://www.cs.rhul.ac.uk/home/uxac009/files/papers/tocl17.pdf','Inherited author manuscript; ACM Transactions on Computational Logic 18(3), article 25; DOI 10.1145/3091122; definitions and final open direction (2), manuscript p. 40'),ref('logical','Higher-Order Recursion Schemes and Collapsible Pushdown Automata: Logical Properties','Christopher H. Broadbent; Arnaud Carayol; C.-H. Luke Ong; Olivier Serre',2021,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.06366v2','Posted October 2020, revised 2 March 2021; effective model checking, reflection and selection'),ref('horn','Reducing Higher-order Recursion Scheme Equivalence to Coinductive Higher-order Constrained Horn Clauses','Jerome Jochems',2021,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.04632','HCVS 2021, EPTCS 344, pp. 36–64; §2.2 defines semantics and the ⊥-free transform; §4 gives a conditional route via open semidecidability'),ref('polyregular','Polyregular equivalence is undecidable in higher-order types','Mikołaj Bojańczyk; Grzegorz Fabiański; Rafał Stefański',2026,'https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.11935','13 April 2026; §§1–3, higher-order extensional equivalence for a list-processing λ-calculus, a different model'),ref('probabilistic','On Higher-Order Probabilistic Verification via the Weighted Relational Model of Linear Logic','Ugo Dal Lago; Guido Fiorillo; Paolo Pistone',2026,'https://drops.dagstuhl.de/entities/document/10.4230/LIPIcs.LICS.2026.34','LICS 2026; arXiv:2604.27986 posted 30 April 2026; almost-sure termination for a subclass defined using bounded exponentials')],
 status='source_open',status_note='Open for unrestricted finite-order deterministic schemes with the exact tree semantics stated here, following the inspected primary sources and a later-result search through 11 September 2026. Conditional Horn reductions, model checking, probabilistic termination and higher-order polyregular functional equivalence do not by themselves settle it.',formulation_reviewed_on='2026-09-11')
